  "logs": {
    "format": "JSON lines, one event per line",
    "required_fields": ["timestamp", "level", "service", "request_id", "route", "status", "duration_ms"],
    "rules": ["request_id generated per request and echoed in the response header", "never log passwords, tokens, or full request bodies", "errors log the exception type and message, not just 'failed'"]
  },
  "metrics": [
    {"name": "http_requests_total", "labels": ["route", "status"], "purpose": "traffic and error-rate by endpoint"},
    {"name": "http_request_duration_ms", "type": "histogram", "purpose": "p50/p95 latency, not just averages"},
    {"name": "db_query_duration_ms", "type": "histogram", "purpose": "catch slow queries before users do"},
    {"name": "jobs_failed_total", "purpose": "background job health"}
  ],
  "alerts": [
    {"name": "high_error_rate", "condition": "5xx > 2% of requests over 10 minutes", "severity": "page"},
    {"name": "latency_regression", "condition": "p95 > 800ms over 30 minutes", "severity": "ticket"},
    {"name": "job_silence", "condition": "nightly job produced no success event by 06:00", "severity": "page"}
  ],
  "maintainability_note": "Three alerts only. Every alert must have a runbook line saying what to do; an alert nobody acts on is worse than none."
}
```

The deliberate restraint is the design: most small teams drown in dashboards they never open. A request_id in every log line plus three alerts with thresholds tied to user pain covers 90% of real incidents.

